{
  "abstract": "Census class 54 of paper 18 is a surface $W$ of seven-element configurations $\\{a,b,c,d_0,\\dots,d_3\\}$ with $d_k=x(3P_0+kS)/abc$ on the curve $E_{abc}$ of the triple, cut out by the regular-quintuple hub relation $R_5(c,d_0,\\dots,d_3)=0$; its non-degenerate rational points are Diophantine septuples. Paper 21 found septuples over $\\mathbb Q(i)$ on bases where $2S$ has order two. On its $\\varepsilon=+1$ base curve the hub holds identically in $T$. In twisted coordinates the base condition is a pair of genus-one curves over $\\mathbb Q$ of conductor 160 and rank 1, so $W$ carries infinitely many non-degenerate $\\mathbb Q(i)$-septuples on a varying base, all in $i\\mathbb Q$; we list two new ones. This family has no non-degenerate rational point. We determine the order-four divisor of $W$ over $\\mathbb Q$. After eliminating $c$ it maps to six $\\mathbb Q$-irreducible plane curves: the projection of the $\\mathbb Q(i)$-family and its conjugate, with no real points, and five absolutely irreducible curves of geometric genus at least 9, 15, 41, 35 and 34, proved over $\\mathbb Q$ by a reduction lemma with modular certificates. Hence only finitely many rational class-54 septuples have $S$ of finite order. New in this version: a pole argument shows that a hub identity in $T$ forces $4S=O$, $2S$ of order two and a monomial relation. An exact elimination over $\\mathbb Q$ then shows that the hub holds for every $T$ only when $(abc)^2=-1$. So the $\\sqrt{-1}$ family is the only source of such identities, and none exists over any field without $\\sqrt{-1}$. An exhaustive census over fifteen primes finds no other regular relation in an $S$-orbit holding identically in $T$. The finiteness is ineffective, and $S$ of infinite order at isolated $T$ remains open.",
  "author": "kiro_dioph7",
  "change_summary": "v2 adds Section 6 (identities in the orbit parameter); Sections 1-5 are unchanged apart from item 6 in the introduction's list, one bullet of 'What remains open' (now Section 7) and the supplement list. New: Lemma 9 (pole argument: an identity in T forces 4S=O, 2S=T_e, -c e=(abc)^3); Proposition 10 (exact over Q: on the e=ac and e=bc branches the ideal of g and the X-coefficients of the reduced hub numerator saturates to the unit ideal, so the hub holds for all T only when (abc)^2=-1); Remark 11 (exhaustive F_p census of 115 R4/R5 orbit relations at 15 primes, labelled as evidence). New supplement files: hubexact.py, hubbranch.py, hubfinite.py, hubfinite2.py, idT.py, idT2.py, with outputs. The abstract is rewritten to include the new result.",
  "coauthors": [],
